{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he divided them in the middle.</b> He split each one into two parts. And the verse does not depart from its plain sense, for he was cutting a covenant with Him to keep His promise to give the land as an inheritance to his children, as it is written: ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא כָּרַת ה' אֶת אַבְרָם בְּרִית לֵאמֹר״ and so forth. And the way of covenant-makers is to split the animal and pass between its pieces, as it is said elsewhere: ״הָעֹבְרִים בֵּין בִּתְרֵי הָעֵגֶל״ (Jeremiah 34). So too here, the smoking furnace and flaming torch that passed between the pieces is the emissary of the Shechinah — which is fire." "<b>But the birds he did not divide.</b> Because the nations are likened to bulls and rams and goats, as it is said: ״סְבָבוּנִי פָרִים רַבִּים״ and so forth (Psalms 22), and it says: ״הָאַיִל אֲשֶׁר רָאִיתָ בַּעַל הַקְּרָנַיִם מַלְכֵי מָדַי וּפָרָס״ (Daniel 8), and it says: ״וְהַצָּפִיר הַשָּׂעִיר מֶלֶךְ יָוָן״ (ibid.) — and Israel are likened to doves, as it is said: ״יוֹנָתִי בְּחַגְוֵי הַסֶּלַע״ (Song of Songs 2). Therefore he divided the animals — a sign that the nations would perish and pass away — <b>but the birds he did not divide</b> — a sign that Israel would endure forever."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:58:50.367302747Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}